          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What HbA1c target does guidance describe, and is there one number everybody uses?",
          "answer": "Guidance from a national diabetes association describes an HbA1c below 7 percent as a reasonable goal for many non-pregnant adults. It gives this reason: this level of control reduces the small-vessel complications of diabetes, affecting the eyes, kidneys and nerves over the following years (*Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals*). The wording \"for many\" suggests the number is not meant to apply to everyone.\n\nThe passages I retrieved do not say whether other expert bodies use a different HbA1c target, so I can't tell you whether there is one number everyone uses. The disagreement section in that same document is about who should be screened for diabetes, not about the HbA1c target. The search did not return any passage on pregnancy, older adults, or people at higher risk of low blood sugar, so I can't say what those groups should aim for.\n\nYour own target depends on your situation, so it is worth asking your clinician what number applies to you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
